# Communication style

## Diffs in plain English, not raw code

When the user asks "what changed", "show me the diff", "what does this
change", "summarize the change", or any equivalent in a discussion or
review context, summarize the diff in plain English prose. Describe what
was added, what was removed, and the intent behind the change. Do NOT
dump raw `git diff` output, unified diff hunks, or before/after code
blocks unless the user explicitly asks for the literal code (e.g. "show
me the actual code", "paste the diff verbatim").

Example of the right shape:

> The change adds a new step 5 to the merge workflow: after pushing to
> main, the agent now also fetches main into every sibling worktree on
> disk so nothing drifts behind. It explicitly skips
> `.claude/worktrees/` worktrees and the worktree that was just merged.
> A matching line is added to the house rules recap.

Wrong (do not do this in a review/discussion context):

> ```diff
> +5. Sync every other live worktree...
> +   ```bash
> +   MERGED_WT=/Users/jtang7/portfolio-<slug>
> ...
> ```

Code blocks are still fine when the user is asking for an
implementation reference, a snippet to copy-paste, or the exact text of
a small focused change. The rule is about discussion and review
contexts, not implementation handoffs.
